ng the same theme that's  been shared so far  great and claire could you also chime in  if there's anything else to add in terms  of  what surprised you and any tips in your  first year that you learned  yeah um probably two big things one  um i had a really hard time  with the fact that it felt like i had  gone from this place where everything i  did was so other people could be  successful  to being like only worried about myself  and grad school is just like very  self-indulgent and that's wonderful and  i think i just it took me a little while  to  name that and feel okay about it um  and i think once i sort of was like this  is just a different time in your life  and you're  here to learn so that you can be more  useful to people in the future  um that was the sort of helpful mindset  shift for me  um but i think because of what these  folks have said around just being so  busy it's easy to sort of  be distracted and not not give yourself  enough time to  pay attention to kind of how you're  feeling about the transition um  and then the second thing is that i  found the core classes really hard  and i was acutely aware of what good  teaching looked like and i was sort of  like  lectures are really hard for me to learn  in and i don't know that  i'm absorbing all the sort of financial  literacy that i came here to learn  and a lot of folks in the class know a  lot more than i do  and so i felt a little bit insecure  actually in the beginning  and and i think over time  started to feel like okay this is part  of a larger experience i'm learning  these things they're new to me that's  okay and  also some of the other people management  things and eq things that maybe i have  more exposure to  in managing other adults or classrooms  or things like that  um are gonna be relevant at some point  and so i think it was just sort of being  okay with  um you know being challenged and  feeling like um  i i think if if i were to  give some advice around that it's just  sort